\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $X$ be smooth and projective over a finite field, let $r\geq0$, and let $\ell$ differ from the field characteristic. Must every Frobenius-invariant class in $H^{2r}_{\mathrm{et}}(X_{\overline{\mathbb F}_q},\mathbb Q_\ell(r))$ be a $\mathbb Q_\ell$-linear combination of cycle classes of codimension-$r$ algebraic cycles defined over the finite field? Applying the assertion after finite field extensions gives the usual Tate-class formulation.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.1090/pspum/055.1/1265523}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $X$ be smooth and projective over a finite field, let $r\geq0$, and let $\ell$ differ from the field characteristic. Must every Frobenius-invariant class in $H^{2r}_{\mathrm{et}}(X_{\overline{\mathbb F}_q},\mathbb Q_\ell(r))$ be a $\mathbb Q_\ell$-linear combination of cycle classes of codimension-$r$ algebraic cycles defined over the finite field? Applying the assertion after finite field extensions gives the usual Tate-class formulation.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1173 (AG-004). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.1090/pspum/055.1/1265523}.
\end{itemize}
